.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1213 PM PDT Sun Oct 4 2026
(This evening through Monday night)

It`s hot. A persistent ridge over the western United States keeps
the area much above the seasonal average (15 to 25 degrees above
seasonal average away from the immediate coast). As of 11 AM today
temperatures across the region ranged from the 80s into the lower
90s inland, the 70s to the lower 80s close to the Bays, and the
middle 60s to the middle 70s for the Pacific coast. There are some
high clouds flowing above the SF Bay Area, but they do not seem to
be impacting the heat levels. High temperatures today and Monday
will be rather similar in the inland valleys, ranging from the
middle 90s to near 104. Closer to the Bays, today`s highs will be in
the middle 80s to the middle 90s while locations along the Pacific
coast rise into the 70s. Across the higher elevations and some
nearby valleys, especially in the East and South Bays, mild
overnight lows in the upper 60s to middle 70s will limit the
ability to recover overnight. For Monday, these temperatures are a
little uncertain owing to potential impacts from a shallow marine
layer or a marginally stronger onshore flow than the current
forecast, but highs should remain in the 70s and 80s. If confidence
in highs towards the higher part of that range increases, the Heat
Advisory for our coastal regions may need to be extended.

Due to the hazardous heat levels, a Heat Advisory is in effect for
coastal locations through 10 PM tonight, while inland and Bayshore
areas remains under the Advisory through Wednesday. Widespread
Moderate HeatRisk blankets the region, corresponding to a moderate
risk of heat-related illnesses for sensitive populations, including
children, the elderly, pregnant women, people with chronic
illnesses, and people who work or live outdoors without access to
adequate cooling or hydration. In addition, many of the higher
elevations in addition to the East Bay, South Bay, and the Carmel,
Hollister, and northern Salinas Valleys see Major HeatRisk,
corresponding to a high risk for heat-related illnesses for anyone
without hydration or cooling. Follow these tips to avoid heat
exhaustion and heatstroke:

* Stay hydrated and drink plenty of fluids.
* Wear lightweight, light-colored clothing.
* Reduce time spent outdoors or stay in the shade.
* Never leave people or pets unattended in vehicles.
* Use sunscreen if going to the coast or the pool.

